From: meta-rpi5-uefi <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] FdtDxe: pick the OS devicetree by board revision

0017 taught FdtDxe to load the OS's own devicetree off a filesystem, but at
one fixed leaf name -- bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb -- on the grounds that guessing
between the Pi 5 variants meant reimplementing the VPU bootloader's
model-to-filename mapping. That reasoning was about the wrong mapping. We are
not naming a tree for the bootloader to find; we are choosing between trees
somebody already laid down, in mainline's naming, which is a table U-Boot has
carried for years and which this SoC family has exactly one interesting row
in.

Raspberry Pi 5 rev 1.1 is BCM2712 D0, and D0's pinctrl register offsets are
not C0's. Mainline calls that tree bcm2712-d-rpi-5-b.dtb. Asking for
bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb on a D0 board hands the OS a C0 tree, which boots -- that
is what makes it hard to notice -- with every pinctrl offset wrong. The same
failure the talos-boot-dtbs recipe already goes out of its way to keep the VPU
bootloader out of, arriving instead through FdtDxe. That recipe deploys both
trees under by-uname/<release>/, so on the cards this firmware ships the right
one is already sitting next to the wrong one; nothing was asking for it.

BoardRevisionHelperLib gains BoardRevisionGetFdtName(), a sibling of
BoardRevisionGetModelName() driven off the same RPI_TYPE switch and carrying
the same models. Where a model needs more than one tree it gets a list indexed
by the revision nibble with entry 0 as the default, which is U-Boot's
rpi_models_new_scheme[] FDTFILES() shape and set_fdtfile()'s lookup, one for
one. Unlike its neighbours it declines old-scheme revision codes rather than
misdecoding them: those functions produce display strings, this one produces a
file name that gets loaded.

FdtDxe then tries the board's tree first and the old fixed name second, under
both the keyed \dtb\<kernel release>\ and unkeyed \dtb\ directories. The
fallback keeps installs made against 0017 booting; taking it is the
wrong-silicon case above, so it logs a warning rather than substituting
quietly. On a rev 1.0 board both names are the same and only one lookup
happens.

Both files are upstream, which is why this is a patch rather than part of the
overlay.

Signed-off-by: meta-rpi5-uefi <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
diff -urN a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/FdtDxe/FdtDxe.c b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/FdtDxe/FdtDxe.c
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/FdtDxe/FdtDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/FdtDxe/FdtDxe.c
@@ -97,8 +97,8 @@
 //
 // Two shapes are looked for:
 //
-//   \dtb\<kernel release>\bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb   -- keyed to one kernel
-//   \dtb\bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb                    -- whatever boots
+//   \dtb\<kernel release>\<tree>   -- keyed to one kernel
+//   \dtb\<tree>                    -- whatever boots
 //
 // The keyed form is there because "the tree that matches the kernel" stops
 // being a single answer on a machine that keeps two kernels around. An A/B
@@ -110,16 +110,34 @@
 // `uname -r` string such as "6.18.44-talos", so the two are paired by
 // construction rather than by an installer remembering to keep them in step.
 //
-// One fixed leaf name, deliberately. Guessing between the Pi 5 variants
-// (bcm2712-rpi-5-b / bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b / bcm2712-d-rpi-5-b) from a board
-// revision would put us in the business of reimplementing the VPU
-// bootloader's model-to-filename mapping, and getting it wrong hands the OS a
-// tree for different silicon. Placing the right file is the installer's job,
-// exactly as it is for config.txt's device_tree=.
-//
-#define FDT_OS_LEAF      L"bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb"
-#define FDT_OS_PATH      L"\\dtb\\" FDT_OS_LEAF
-#define FDT_OS_PATH_FMT  L"\\dtb\\%a\\" FDT_OS_LEAF
+// <tree> is the board's, not a constant. BoardRevisionGetFdtName() maps the
+// revision code to the tree that board wants, off the same table U-Boot
+// drives its `fdtfile` from (board/raspberrypi/rpi/rpi.c: rpi_model.fdtfiles,
+// indexed by the revision nibble in set_fdtfile()). The Pi 5 needs it: rev
+// 1.1 is D0 silicon, whose pinctrl register offsets are not C0's, and
+// mainline calls that tree bcm2712-d-rpi-5-b. Asking for one fixed name hands
+// a D0 board the C0 tree, which boots -- that is what makes it hard to notice
+// -- and is wrong.
+//
+// Names are mainline's, matching what an OS installs next to its kernel. The
+// VPU bootloader's own naming is a separate scheme (it calls the D0 tree
+// bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b) and is not reimplemented here; this is only ever
+// choosing between files somebody else laid down.
+//
+// FDT_OS_LEAF_FALLBACK is tried after the board's own name so an install made
+// while this did ask for one fixed name still boots. Taking it means running
+// a C0 tree on D0 silicon, so it is a warning rather than a quiet
+// substitution.
+//
+#define FDT_OS_LEAF_FALLBACK   "bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb"
+#define FDT_OS_PATH_FMT        L"\\dtb\\%a"
+#define FDT_OS_PATH_FMT_UNAME  L"\\dtb\\%a\\%a"
+
+//
+// Longest tree name we will build a path out of. The table's longest today is
+// 26 characters (bcm2712-rpi-cm5l-cm5io.dtb).
+//
+#define FDT_OS_LEAF_MAX  32
 
 //
 // Longest kernel release we will build a path out of. Linux caps its own
@@ -832,6 +850,79 @@
 }
 
 /**
+  Look for the device trees this board can boot, under one directory, and stop
+  at the first that loads.
+
+  The board's own tree is tried first and the model default second; on a Pi 5
+  rev 1.0 those are the same name and only one lookup happens.
+
+  @param  Uname[in]  Kernel release to key the directory on, or NULL for the
+                     unkeyed \dtb\ directory.
+
+  @retval EFI_SUCCESS  A tree was loaded, fixed up and installed.
+  @retval other        No volume carries any of the names.
+
+**/
+STATIC
+EFI_STATUS
+LoadFdtForBoard (
+  IN CONST CHAR8  *Uname   OPTIONAL
+  )
+{
+  CONST CHAR8  *Names[2];
+  UINTN        Count;
+  UINTN        Index;
+  CHAR16       Path[FDT_OS_UNAME_MAX + FDT_OS_LEAF_MAX + 8];
+  EFI_STATUS   Status;
+
+  Count = 0;
+
+  Names[Count] = BoardRevisionGetFdtName (mBoardRevision);
+  if (Names[Count] != NULL) {
+    Count++;
+  }
+
+  //
+  // Only a second entry if it would say something different: an unreadable
+  // revision code leaves the fallback as the sole candidate, which is the
+  // behaviour this had before it could tell the boards apart.
+  //
+  if ((Count == 0) || (AsciiStrCmp (Names[0], FDT_OS_LEAF_FALLBACK) != 0)) {
+    Names[Count] = FDT_OS_LEAF_FALLBACK;
+    Count++;
+  }
+
+  Status = EFI_NOT_FOUND;
+
+  for (Index = 0; Index < Count; Index++) {
+    if (Uname != NULL) {
+      UnicodeSPrint (Path, sizeof (Path), FDT_OS_PATH_FMT_UNAME, Uname, Names[Index]);
+    } else {
+      UnicodeSPrint (Path, sizeof (Path), FDT_OS_PATH_FMT, Names[Index]);
+    }
+
+    Status = LoadFdtByPath (Path);
+    if (!EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+      if (Index > 0) {
+        DEBUG ((
+          DEBUG_WARN,
+          "%a: board revision 0x%x wants '%a', which is not installed; "
+          "booting '%s' instead -- expect the wrong pinctrl offsets\n",
+          __func__,
+          mBoardRevision,
+          Names[0],
+          Path
+          ));
+      }
+
+      break;
+    }
+  }
+
+  return Status;
+}
+
+/**
   At ReadyToBoot, prefer a device tree the OS installed over the one the VPU
   bootloader loaded.
 
@@ -853,7 +944,7 @@
   IN VOID       *Context
   )
 {
-  LoadFdtByPath (FDT_OS_PATH);
+  LoadFdtForBoard (NULL);
 }
 
 /**
@@ -1056,7 +1147,6 @@
   EFI_HANDLE                 Handle;
   UINTN                      BufferSize;
   CHAR8                      Uname[FDT_OS_UNAME_MAX + 1];
-  CHAR16                     Path[FDT_OS_UNAME_MAX + 32];
 
   while (TRUE) {
     BufferSize = sizeof (Handle);
@@ -1099,15 +1189,13 @@
     //
     mOsFdtMatched = TRUE;
 
-    UnicodeSPrint (Path, sizeof (Path), FDT_OS_PATH_FMT, Uname);
-
-    Status = LoadFdtByPath (Path);
+    Status = LoadFdtForBoard (Uname);
     DEBUG ((
       DEBUG_INFO,
-      "%a: booting %a: '%s': %r\n",
+      "%a: booting %a: '\\dtb\\%a\\': %r\n",
       __func__,
       Uname,
-      Path,
+      Uname,
       Status
       ));
   }
diff -urN a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Include/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib.h b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Include/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib.h
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Include/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib.h
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Include/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib.h
@@ -29,6 +29,12 @@
 
 CHAR8 *
 EFIAPI
+BoardRevisionGetFdtName (
+  IN  UINT32  RevisionCode
+  );
+
+CHAR8 *
+EFIAPI
 BoardRevisionGetManufacturerName (
   IN  UINT32  RevisionCode
   );
diff -urN a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib/BoardRevisionHelperLib.c b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib/BoardRevisionHelperLib.c
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib/BoardRevisionHelperLib.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Library/BoardRevisionHelperLib/BoardRevisionHelperLib.c
@@ -16,6 +16,7 @@
 #define RPI_MANUFACTURER(Rev)     ((Rev >> 16) & 0x0F)
 #define RPI_PROCESSOR(Rev)        ((Rev >> 12) & 0x0F)
 #define RPI_TYPE(Rev)             ((Rev >> 4) & 0xFF)
+#define RPI_REVISION(Rev)         (Rev & 0x0F)
 
 UINT64
 EFIAPI
@@ -120,6 +121,131 @@
   return "Unknown Raspberry Pi Model";
 }
 
+//
+// Device tree file names, spelled the way mainline Linux spells them
+// (arch/arm64/boot/dts/broadcom), because that is the name an OS installer
+// has next to its kernel. The Raspberry Pi firmware's own tree names differ
+// for some variants -- it calls the Pi 5 D0 tree bcm2712d0-rpi-5-b -- and are
+// deliberately not what this returns.
+//
+// A model that needs more than one tree gets a list indexed by the board
+// revision nibble, exactly as U-Boot's rpi_models_new_scheme[] FDTFILES()
+// lists are (board/raspberrypi/rpi/rpi.c): entry 0 is the model default, and
+// a revision past the end of the list falls back to it.
+//
+STATIC CONST CHAR8 *CONST  mFdtNamesRpi5B[] = {
+  "bcm2712-rpi-5-b.dtb",       // rev 1.0 -- BCM2712 C0
+  "bcm2712-d-rpi-5-b.dtb"      // rev 1.1 -- BCM2712 D0
+};
+
+/**
+  Pick one entry out of a revision-indexed device tree list.
+
+  @param  Names         The list, model default first.
+  @param  Count         How many entries it has.
+  @param  RevisionCode  The board's revision code.
+
+  @return  The name for this board revision, or the model default if the list
+           does not reach that far.
+
+**/
+STATIC
+CHAR8 *
+FdtNameByRevision (
+  IN  CONST CHAR8 *CONST  *Names,
+  IN  UINTN               Count,
+  IN  UINT32              RevisionCode
+  )
+{
+  UINTN  Revision;
+
+  Revision = RPI_REVISION (RevisionCode);
+  if (Revision >= Count) {
+    Revision = 0;
+  }
+
+  return (CHAR8 *) Names[Revision];
+}
+
+/**
+  The device tree this board wants, as a bare file name.
+
+  @param  RevisionCode  The board's revision code.
+
+  @return  A static string, or NULL if the code names no board we know a tree
+           for. The caller owns nothing.
+
+**/
+CHAR8 *
+EFIAPI
+BoardRevisionGetFdtName (
+  IN  UINT32  RevisionCode
+  )
+{
+  //
+  // Old-scheme codes (bit 23 clear) hold the type in the low byte and index a
+  // different table entirely -- original Pi 1 boards, which no platform here
+  // builds for. Decline them rather than decode them as new-scheme and name a
+  // tree for the wrong board. The functions either side of this one are only
+  // producing display strings and can afford to be loose about it; a file
+  // name gets loaded.
+  //
+  if ((RevisionCode == 0) || ((RevisionCode & BIT23) == 0)) {
+    return NULL;
+  }
+
+  switch (RPI_TYPE (RevisionCode)) {
+    case 0x00:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-a.dtb";
+    case 0x01:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-b.dtb";
+    case 0x02:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-a-plus.dtb";
+    case 0x03:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-b-plus.dtb";
+    case 0x04:
+      return "bcm2836-rpi-2-b.dtb";
+    case 0x06:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-cm.dtb";
+    case 0x08:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-3-b.dtb";
+    case 0x09:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-zero.dtb";
+    case 0x0A:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-cm3.dtb";
+    case 0x0C:
+      return "bcm2835-rpi-zero-w.dtb";
+    case 0x0D:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-3-b-plus.dtb";
+    case 0x0E:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-3-a-plus.dtb";
+    case 0x10:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-cm3.dtb";
+    case 0x11:
+      return "bcm2711-rpi-4-b.dtb";
+    case 0x12:
+      return "bcm2837-rpi-zero-2-w.dtb";
+    case 0x13:
+      return "bcm2711-rpi-400.dtb";
+    case 0x14:
+      return "bcm2711-rpi-cm4.dtb";
+    case 0x17:
+      return FdtNameByRevision (
+               mFdtNamesRpi5B,
+               ARRAY_SIZE (mFdtNamesRpi5B),
+               RevisionCode
+               );
+    case 0x18:
+      return "bcm2712-rpi-cm5-cm5io.dtb";
+    case 0x19:
+      return "bcm2712-rpi-500.dtb";
+    case 0x1A:
+      return "bcm2712-rpi-cm5l-cm5io.dtb";
+  }
+
+  return NULL;
+}
+
 CHAR8 *
 EFIAPI
 BoardRevisionGetManufacturerName (
